// STM32G474-class platform model for the CANcestry T2 virtual bench (H-07, issue #53).
//
// Implements: HW-FR-001 (register map matching platform/cortex_m at v1.0.0),
//             HW-SF-002 (retention domain: RTC_BKP0R preserved across IWDG reset),
//             HW-SF-004 (QA-EV-01 escalation ordering observable from the platform).
// Governing documents: docs/hw/virtual-bench-plan.md section 3 (model scopes and
// `not_simulated` rule), docs/hw/HW-PLAN.md section 8.3 (virtual bench),
// HwAGENTS.md rules 2, 5 and 6.
//
// Scope (virtual-bench-plan section 3): this platform models EXACTLY the
// register surface used by the v1.0.0 firmware under `platform/cortex_m/`:
//
//   modeled            | because
//   -------------------+-------------------------------------------------------
//   Cortex-M4F + NVIC  | executes the real v1.0.0 firmware ELF (no reimplementation)
//   FLASH/RAM/CCMRAM   | cancestry_baremetal.ld MEMORY map (512K/96K/32K)
//   IWDG @0x40003000   | watchdog.c escalation path (HW-SF-002 (i)); KR 0x5555/
//                      | 0xAAAA/0xCCCC protocol, PR/RLR, LSI/(4*2^PR) time base
//                      | (RM0440: PR=3 -> /32 -> 1 ms ticks at 32 kHz LSI),
//                      | expiry sets RCC_CSR.IWDGRSTF (bit 29) and requests reset
//   RTC/BKP @0x40002800| watchdog.c retention register RTC_BKP0R (offset 0x50),
//                      | value persists across machine reset (HW-SF-002)
//   GPIOA @0x48000000  | watchdog.c safe-latch: BSRR reset of bits 8/9
//   RCC @0x40021000    | reset-cause readback: CSR IWDGRSTF (bit 29), RMVF (bit 23);
//                      | reset-cause flags persist across the scripted IWDG reset
//                      | (trace-area shadow at 0x60000024, as on real STM32G4
//                      | retention domain until RMVF) - H-08 bring-up fix F-5
//   FDCAN1/2 @0x40006400/0x40006800 | register scratch for the HAL bases
//   T2 trace @0x60000000 (virtual, unused FMC bank) | event-capture contract
//                      | with the FMI bridge (see below)
//
// NOT_SIMULATED (with rationale, per virtual-bench-plan section 3):
//   - bxCAN/FDCAN protocol engine, bit timing and bus arbitration: CAN
//     protocol simulation is out of H-07 scope (issue #53, constraint 5) and
//     REMAINS out of scope at H-10 (issue #62). The H-10 fault scenarios do
//     not add a protocol engine to this file: the CAN bus fault injector
//     (renode/can_fault_injector.py) is an EXTENSION registered by
//     renode/cancestry-hw-fault.resc at 0x40000000, and it projects the
//     fault CONDITION (PSR.BO / PSR.LEC / ECR / IR.ELO / CCCR.INIT) onto the
//     FDCAN register scratch declared below. Frame delivery, arbitration and
//     the physical layer stay not_simulated, and the fault scenarios derive
//     no claim from them (see can_fault_injector.py header for the explicit
//     modeled / not-modeled split);
//   - the BOR reset LINE (H-11, issue #64) is an EXTENSION registered by
//     renode/cancestry-hw-bor.resc at 0x40001000 (bor_reset_injector.py: NRST
//     active-low, RCC_CSR.BORRSTF, main-SRAM marker discard, BOR machine
//     reset), and the brownout plant is the T1 model
//     hw/model/CancestryLib/Power/BOR.mo - see the BOR bullet below;
//   - DWT CYCCNT (0xE0001000) and CoreDebug DEMCR (0xE000EDFC): the Cortex-M
//     core debug block is owned by the Renode CPU model; the firmware's DWT
//     timestamps are NOT load-bearing for the T2 retention ordering (events
//     are captured from emulation virtual time), so DWT accuracy carries the
//     renode TCL2 validation gap (docs/hw/tool-qualification.md section 3).
//     F-27 (issue #55, per Lead SE decision 2026-09-22): verified that the
//     T2 ELF call graph (hw/virtual-bench/firmware/main.c plus the v1.0.0
//     cancestry_watchdog_* / cancestry_event_hard_fault_escalate paths it
//     invokes) never touches DWT/DEMCR - the driver deliberately bypasses
//     cancestry_watchdog_init and hal_stm32_clock_init - so NO DWT extension
//     of this platform is required for T2;
//   - BOR/power supervisor reset on brownout: H-11 (issue #64) adds the RESET
//     LINE as a registered extension (renode/bor_reset_injector.py, registered
//     by renode/cancestry-hw-bor.resc at 0x40001000 - not declared in this
//     file, see the F-28 note below), while the BOR CELL physics stay outside
//     the platform: they are the T1 plant hw/model/CancestryLib/Power/BOR.mo.
//     This file's own behaviour is UNCHANGED by H-11 (comment-only contract
//     update, issue #64 constraint 4). The passive fail-safe topology remains
//     a requirement (HwAGENTS.md rule 6) and every safety-relevant change in
//     this area stays Human-Reviewer gated: H-11 delivers a pending/CL0
//     mechanism and promotes nothing. The brownout energy margin itself is
//     closed at T1 by OR-001 (Holdup FMU);
//   - all remaining STM32G4 peripherals (DMA, ADC, DAC, USART, SPI, I2C,
//     timers, USB, etc.): untouched by platform/cortex_m at v1.0.0.
//
// Event-capture contract (consumed by hw/virtual-bench/fmi_bridge.py):
//
//   0x60000000  magic      0x54324353 (T2CS), written by cancestry-hw.resc
//   0x60000004  flags      reserved (0)
//   0x60000008  retention_write_us   cpu symbol hook:
//               cancestry_watchdog_write_retention_register
//   0x6000000C  safe_latch_us        cpu symbol hook:
//               cancestry_hardware_set_safe_state
//   0x60000010  escalation_us        cpu symbol hook:
//               cancestry_watchdog_escalate
//   0x60000014  iwdg_fire_us         iwdg_model.py at expiry
//   0x60000020  vbat_mv_in           written by the FMI bridge (plant -> MCU)
//   0x60000024  rcc_csr_shadow       persistence store for RCC_CSR; the RCC
//               reset-cause flags survive the scripted IWDG reset here (same
//               trace-area pattern as the retention shadow), as on real
//               STM32G4 until RMVF is written
//   0x60000100  retention_shadow     persistence store for RTC_BKP0R; lives in
//               the (non-volatile across machine reset) trace area so the
//               retained fault code survives the scripted IWDG reset
//
// H-10 CAN fault-capture contract (issue #62, HW-FR-003; installed by
// renode/cancestry-hw-fault.resc only - the retention scenario never touches
// these slots and their writers are declared here so the one-writer-per-slot
// rule is reviewable). All values are integer microseconds of emulation
// virtual time; 0 means "did not occur":
//
//   0x60000040  bus_off_inject_us    can_fault_injector.py: InjectBusOff accepted
//   0x60000044  crc_inject_us        can_fault_injector.py: InjectCRCError accepted
//   0x60000048  recovery_request_us  cancestry-hw-fault.resc CCCR write hook
//               (firmware cleared CCCR.INIT while the medium was Bus-Off: the
//               ISO 11898-1 / RM0440 Bus-Off recovery request)
//   0x6000004c  bus_active_us        can_fault_injector.py: medium released
//               after the 128 x 11 recessive-bit sequence (704 us at 2 Mbit/s)
//   0x60000050  crc_cleared_us       can_fault_injector.py: CRC condition left
//               the medium after the acknowledge at 0x60000054
//   0x60000054  crc_ack_us           cancestry-hw-fault.resc IR write hook
//               (firmware acknowledged the error-logging interrupt, IR.ELO)
//   0x60000058  busoff_detect_us     cpu symbol hook: t2_can_busoff_detect
//   0x6000005c  busoff_recover_us    cpu symbol hook: t2_can_busoff_recover
//   0x60000060  crc_detect_us        cpu symbol hook: t2_can_crc_detect
//   0x60000064  crc_error_count      firmware (off-tree CAN fault driver)
//   0x60000068  run_complete         firmware: 1 when the scenario loop ended
//               normally (no crash, no park)
//   0x6000006c  alive_counter        firmware: increments every poll pass, so a
//               hung firmware is visible as a stopped counter
//
// H-11 brownout/BOR-capture contract (issue #64, HW-SF-002 (ii)/(iii),
// HW-SF-004; installed by renode/cancestry-hw-bor.resc only - the retention
// and CAN fault scenarios never touch these slots, and their writers are
// declared here so the one-writer-per-slot rule is reviewable). All values
// are integer microseconds of emulation virtual time; 0 means "did not
// occur". This is a COMMENT-ONLY contract: no peripheral or memory map is
// changed by H-11 in this file.
//
//   0x60000008  retention_write_us  .resc symbol hook, FIRST-EVENT-ONLY
//               (cancestry_watchdog_write_retention_register): the retained
//               terminal code published BEFORE the brownout
//   0x60000070  bor_inject_us       bor_reset_injector.py: NRST asserted
//               (active-low) at the modelled BOR assertion instant
//   0x60000074  nrst_release_us     bor_reset_injector.py: NRST released
//               exactly 100 us later (the issue's brownout pulse)
//   0x60000078  bor_detect_us       .resc symbol hook: t2_bor_detect, the
//               post-BOR classification entry (reset cause + retained code)
//   0x6000007C  bor_recover_us      .resc symbol hook on the REAL v1.0.0
//               safe-state restoration fn cancestry_hardware_set_safe_state
//   0x60000080  sram_magic_at_boot  firmware: main-SRAM marker word read at
//               the post-BOR boot (0 = the reset discarded main SRAM)
//   0x60000084  bor_run_complete    firmware: 1 once the recovery completed
//   0x60000088  bor_alive_counter   firmware: advances every service pass
//   0x6000008C  retention_code_at_detect  firmware: the retained code
//               recovered through the real v1.0.0 retention read API
//   0x60000104  nrst_level          bor_reset_injector.py: NRST pin level
//               (1 released, 0 asserted) - STATE word, not an event slot
//   0x60000108  sram_canary_lost    bor_reset_injector.py: 1 once the BOR
//               reset discarded the main-SRAM marker word
//
// The main-SRAM marker word itself lives in the SRAM region at 0x20017000
// (T2-declared: inside the 96 KiB RAM of cancestry_baremetal.ld, outside the
// ELF's .data/.bss, > 4 KiB below the initial stack pointer 0x20018000). The
// injector discards it with the BOR reset, so the post-reset firmware can
// report main-SRAM loss while the retention shadow (below) survives.
//
// Determinism (HwAGENTS.md rule 5): this platform is deterministic by
// construction. The FMI bridge drives emulation with fixed-size
// `emulation RunFor` quanta (100 us master step) and `emulation SetSeed`
// fixes the only randomness source. No wall-clock input is consumed.
//
// Note for reviewers: the scripted peripherals (Python.PythonPeripheral,
// IronPython 2) implement register semantics only. Peripheral-script API:
// request.IsInit/IsRead/IsWrite/Value/Offset and self per Renode
// documentation (renode.readthedocs.io, 'Using Python in Renode'). The
// pinned Renode version is checked by the T2 runner before loading this
// platform; on any scripted-peripheral API mismatch the platform fails to
// load and the T2 run aborts (fail-closed).

// CPU: Cortex-M4F. Virtual time is driven exclusively by the FMI bridge's
// fixed `emulation RunFor` quanta. The instruction rate is pinned
// EXPLICITLY at 100 MIPS (bring-up finding F-10; the T2 timing window in
// hw/virtual-bench/firmware/main.c is designed against 100 MIPS).
// DLR subtlety (v1.16.1, source-verified in F-27): a .repl attribute is a
// property assignment IFF its name starts with an UPPERCASE letter - any
// other case is treated as a constructor argument (ConstructorOrProperty
// Attribute.IsPropertyAttribute). 'PerformanceInMips' is a property on
// BaseCPU (the CPU base class; constructor default 100) - the official
// platforms/cpus/stm32f746.repl declares 'PerformanceInMips: 462' in
// uppercase property form. Dispatch 16 (run 35778907099) failed with DLR
// error E25 ('Could not find suitable constructor for ...CortexM')
// because the camelCase 'performanceInMips: 100' was classified as a
// constructor argument with no matching parameter (unused attribute ->
// constructor rejected); F-26 removed the line (harmless - the default is
// 100 - but a misdiagnosis), and F-27 restores it in the property form
// below. 'cpuType: "cortex-m4f"' (FPU enabled) is the official idiom for
// M4F (platforms/cpus/stm32f412.repl, v1.16.1). 'systickFrequency' on the
// NVIC is a genuine constructor argument (lowercase is correct there);
// the 170 MHz systick base (STM32G474 main clock) is modeled but unused -
// the T2 driver never enables systick. 'IRQ -> cpu@0' is the canonical
// NVIC-to-CPU interrupt line (official stm32f4.repl / stm32f746.repl);
// the T2 driver raises no interrupts, so it is inert here.
nvic: IRQControllers.NVIC @ sysbus 0xE000E000
    systickFrequency: 170000000
    IRQ -> cpu@0

cpu: CPU.CortexM @ sysbus
    cpuType: "cortex-m4f"
    PerformanceInMips: 100
    nvic: nvic

// Memory per platform/cortex_m/cancestry_baremetal.ld (MEMORY block).
flash: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x08000000
    size: 0x80000

sram: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x20000000
    size: 0x18000

ccmram: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x10000000
    size: 0x8000

// IWDG: scripted model, see header. Expiry is evaluated lazily on bus
// access; the co-simulation master polls after every RunFor quantum, which
// bounds the fire-timestamp quantization by the master step while the hook
// timestamps above stay exact.
//
// F-28 (issue #55): the four scripted register models below (IWDG,
// RTC/BKP, GPIOA, RCC) are NOT declared in this file - cancestry-hw.resc
// creates them via `machine PyDevFromFile $ORIGIN/<file> ...`. Reason:
// PythonPeripheral resolves a bare `filename:` CWD-relative IN ITS
// CONSTRUCTOR (File.Exists, PythonPeripheral.cs, v1.16.1), and headless
// CI's effective process CWD is not this script's directory (dispatch 17,
// run 35805300102: 'Could not find source file for the script:
// iwdg_model.py' at this file's iwdg line; dispatch 14, run 35773043927:
// the same CWD behavior broke the .resc load until the $ORIGIN fix).
// $ORIGIN-absolute paths remove the CWD dependence (Lead SE pre-authorized
// absolute/$ORIGIN paths, 2026-09-22). Addresses/sizes/initable are
// unchanged from the previous inline declarations:
//   iwdg       0x40003000 size 0x14 initable
//   rtc_backup 0x40002800 size 0x64
//   gpioa      0x48000000 size 0x400 initable
//   rcc        0x40021000 size 0x98 initable

// FDCAN register scratch for the HAL bases (hal_stm32.c FDCAN1_BASE /
// FDCAN2_BASE). Protocol engine not_simulated (see header).
fdcan1_scratch: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x40006400
    size: 0x400

fdcan2_scratch: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x40006800
    size: 0x400

// T2 event-capture area (virtual; unused FMC bank on this board).
// F-29 (issue #55): size 0x400, NOT the previous 0x200. Dispatch 18
// (run 35806027349) failed with 'Error E39: Could not register memory at
// offset 0x60000000 and size 0x200 - the size has to be aligned to guest
// page size 0x400' (MappedMemory registration, v1.16.1). The header
// layout above ends at 0x60000104 (retention_shadow word at
// 0x60000100), which one 0x400 guest page starting at 0x60000000 fully
// covers (through 0x600003FF); nothing else is mapped in this virtual
// FMC-bank region, so the extra half-page is unused address space only.
t2_trace: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x60000000
    size: 0x400

// F-25 (issue #55): the previous 'sysbus: init: Tag <range> "name"' block
// was removed. CORRECTION (F-27, source-verified against v1.16.1): the
// block was NOT a syntax fault - 'sysbus: init: Tag' IS valid in v1.16.1
// (the official platforms/cpus/stm32f412.repl uses exactly that form).
// Dispatch 15 (run 35777946390) failed with the same E25 constructor
// error on the 'cpu: CPU.CortexM @ sysbus' line that dispatch 16 did -
// the camelCase 'performanceInMips' attribute, diagnosed in F-27 - which
// the truncated diagnostics of the time misattributed to the Tag block.
// The removal stands on its own merits: the range labels were decorative,
// the ranges are documented in the header table above, and the FMI bridge
// (the only consumer) addresses them directly.
